{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is an MLOps production pipeline?

An MLOps production pipeline is the system that turns a trained model into a
repeatable, observable production decision. It connects versioned data and feature
definitions to training, evaluation, approval, gradual deployment, monitoring,
rollback, and retraining.

In plain language: software delivery ships code; MLOps delivery ships a **tested
decision contract**. The contract includes the model weights, but also the data
snapshot, feature definitions, preprocessing, thresholds, serving configuration, and
evidence used to approve the release.

{% callout variant="info" %}
### Core invariant

Never route a request to a model unless its request schema, feature values,
preprocessing, artifact, and decision policy are compatible and traceable to one
approved release. A fast prediction with the wrong features is not a successful
prediction.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

This case study designs an enterprise pipeline for **100+ production models**, up to
**1M predictions per second**, **10 TB of source data per day**, and a **four-hour**
daily retraining window. For serving foundations, review
[model serving](/ml-systems/model-serving) before treating inference as an isolated
model-runtime problem.

{% accordion defaultOpen="clarifying" %}

{% accordion-item id="clarifying" title="1. Clarify the product contract and its failure cost" %}

## The pipeline exists to make production changes reversible

The platform serves fraud, ranking, forecasting, and operations models. These models
have different quality metrics, but every team needs the same platform guarantees:
an approved artifact can be reproduced, released in a bounded cohort, observed with
versioned telemetry, and replaced quickly when its contract no longer holds.

### Functional requirements

- Trigger training from approved data arrivals, schedules, or a reviewed manual request.
- Validate raw inputs and feature definitions before they are used for training or online inference.
- Register model artifacts with owners, lineage, evaluation evidence, and approval state.
- Support shadow, canary, and full deployment for real-time and batch consumers.
- Detect data drift, quality regression, bias regressions, and serving failures.
- Roll back to a compatible prior release and create a retraining work item when evidence fails.

### Non-functional requirements

- Keep the critical online path below **100 ms P99** and available at **99.9%** or better.
- Retain complete release lineage for audits and incident investigation.
- Complete the standard daily training workload within **four hours** without starving online serving.
- Keep online features fresh enough for their product contract, with an explicit stale or missing state.
- Isolate tenants, models, experiments, and rollout cohorts so one failure cannot consume shared capacity.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
### Scope boundary

The platform automates evidence collection and safe mechanical actions. It does not
silently decide that a new objective, a changed label definition, or a material
fairness trade-off is acceptable; those remain accountable human decisions.
{% /callout %}
{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="scale" title="2. Convert the workload into capacity and evidence budgets" %}

## Average load is not the operating envelope

At one million predictions per second, a one-percent canary still serves **10,000
requests per second**. The initial cohort must be large enough to produce useful
evidence and small enough that an incorrect model, feature, or policy cannot affect
the entire product.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="10 TB/day" label="Raw source data" detail="Validated before feature and training jobs consume it" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="1M/s" label="Peak predictions" detail="The request path needs bounded dependencies and headroom" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric value="50/day" label="Training jobs" detail="Scheduled across shared accelerators and data windows" tone="amber" /%}
{% metric value="2 min" label="Rollback target" detail="A compatible prior release stays immediately deployable" tone="green" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

### Capacity implications

- **Data plane:** 10 TB per day becomes larger after validation, intermediate features, replicas, and retained training snapshots. Partition data by time and tenant; do not make a daily retrain depend on one mutable table.
- **Training plane:** reserve or queue approximately 500 GPU-hours per day for normal work, then protect the four-hour service-level objective with priority and per-team quotas.
- **Serving plane:** size for the peak, not the daily average. Feature lookups, queueing, serialization, and policy evaluation all consume the same 100 ms deadline as model execution.
- **Evidence plane:** monitor by model version, cohort, feature version, region, and important population slice. An aggregate accuracy metric cannot prove a canary is safe.

{% callout variant="tip" %}
### Useful release math

At 1M requests per second, a 5% canary receives 50K requests per second. That is a
large operational load, so guardrails must be evaluated continuously and be able to
pause traffic before the analysis window closes.
{% /callout %}
{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="architecture" title="3. Separate the data, control, and decision planes" %}

## Keep the synchronous decision path small

The data and control planes can retry, queue, and recompute. The online decision
path cannot: it has one caller deadline. Separating the planes prevents a slow
backfill, dashboard, or experiment from becoming a user-facing outage.

{% system-flow title="MLOps production pipeline" caption="Offline jobs create approved releases; the online path serves one compatible decision and records evidence asynchronously." %}
{% system-node title="Versioned data and features" eyebrow="Data plane" icon="database" tone="blue" %}
Validate schemas, retain point-in-time snapshots, and publish immutable feature definitions for offline and online use.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Train and evaluate" eyebrow="Build plane" icon="cpu" tone="violet" %}
Run isolated jobs, compare the candidate with a baseline, and produce evaluation evidence by important slice.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Approve and orchestrate" eyebrow="Control plane" icon="shield" tone="amber" %}
Store lineage, approvals, rollout state, policies, and the compatible rollback release.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Serve and observe" eyebrow="Decision plane" icon="activity" tone="green" %}
Fetch compatible online features, execute the pinned artifact, apply policy, and emit non-blocking telemetry.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Ownership boundaries

- **Data producers** own source semantics, freshness commitments, and schema-change notices.
- **Feature owners** own transformations, point-in-time correctness, online/offline compatibility, and deprecation plans.
- **Model teams** own objectives, evaluation slices, acceptance thresholds, and interpretation of business outcomes.
- **Platform operators** own isolation, artifact integrity, rollout mechanics, observability, and the reliability of the shared controls.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="feature-contract" title="4. Make data and features reproducible" %}

## A feature is a versioned production dependency

Training-serving skew occurs when the model was trained with feature logic or values
that cannot be reproduced at prediction time. The usual symptoms look like a model
regression, but the underlying fault may be a changed join, a missing default, a
timezone conversion, or a freshness violation.

### Require this feature contract

- **Point-in-time training data:** create each training row using only values that existed at that event time. This prevents future information from leaking into evaluation.
- **One definition, two stores:** use the same versioned transformation for offline training data and online retrieval. The storage engines may differ; the semantic definition may not.
- **Explicit freshness and null policy:** record whether a feature is fresh, delayed, unavailable, or defaulted. Do not turn missing context into an unmarked zero.
- **Compatibility checks:** reject an incompatible feature schema before it reaches a serving model or a retraining job.

{% callout variant="info" %}
### Example failure

If a fraud model trained on a seven-day purchase total receives an online value built
with a rolling calendar week, the request may be fast and schema-valid while the
meaning has changed. The platform must block that release or return a declared
fallback; it must not call this a model-quality issue after the fact.
{% /callout %}
{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="release" title="5. Release a model only after layered evidence" %}

## A registry is a governed state machine, not a file bucket

An experiment becomes deployable only after the platform binds it to its inputs and
evidence. The registry entry should identify the artifact digest, code revision,
feature version, training snapshot, evaluation report, owner, approval, and the
prior compatible version.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Validate inputs" label="Data and code" %}
Check schema, freshness, feature compatibility, dependency locks, and reproducible training inputs before costly work begins.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Train and compare" label="Offline evidence" %}
Compare the candidate to the baseline on holdout data, critical slices, calibration, fairness checks, cost, and latency on target hardware.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Shadow then canary" label="Online evidence" %}
Observe production-shaped inputs without changing outcomes, then shift a representative cohort while service and model guardrails remain active.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Promote or roll back" label="Controlled change" %}
Promote only when evidence holds; otherwise freeze the candidate, retain its diagnostics, and restore the previously approved compatible release.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Release gates that must all pass

1. **Artifact gate:** image, dependencies, schemas, feature version, and signatures are verified.
2. **Offline quality gate:** the candidate meets absolute floors and does not regress important slices beyond agreed bounds.
3. **Serving gate:** latency, error rate, saturation, memory, and feature freshness meet the deployment envelope.
4. **Online outcome gate:** delayed labels, user behavior, safety signals, and business metrics stay within the canary policy.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Do not promote because the overall offline metric improved. A candidate that raises
aggregate AUC while harming a protected or high-value slice can be a regression.
{% /callout %}
{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="serving" title="6. Protect the feature-serving path at peak traffic" %}

## Verify the release contract before traffic moves

Production readiness is a decision across evidence, not a deployment button. Work
through the release record below as if this were a candidate about to receive its
first canary cohort. Each check changes the visible verification total and persists
locally, so an unchecked item is an explicit reason to hold the release.

{% interactive-checklist
   checklistId="mlops-production-release-gates"
   dataFile="/api/content/case-studies/mlops-production-pipeline/data/release-readiness.json" /%}

### Keep feature fallback bounded

- Coalesce identical cache misses so a hot entity does not stampede the authoritative feature store.
- Cap fallback concurrency and shed low-priority requests before the store queues become unbounded.
- Return an explicit stale, default, smaller-model, abstention, or review result only when the product policy permits it.
- Record the fallback reason with the model and feature versions so degraded behavior is measurable.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="operations" title="7. Turn monitoring into a reversible operating loop" %}

## Observe the model, not just the servers

Healthy CPU and HTTP metrics do not prove that a model is still making useful
decisions. The operating loop joins versioned predictions and exposures to delayed
outcomes, while separately watching data quality, feature freshness, service health,
and business guardrails.

Use the topology to compare a healthy release, a feature-drift signal, a quality
regression, a monitoring backlog, and a registry failure. Selecting a scenario
changes the active path and result; selecting a component reveals its responsibility.

{% topology-lab dataFile="/api/content/case-studies/mlops-production-pipeline/data/release-operations-topology.json" /%}

### Alert policy

- **Page immediately:** invalid artifact signature, incompatible schema, critical fairness or safety breach, or an automatic rollback trigger.
- **Freeze promotion and investigate:** drift, calibration loss, delayed-label regression, elevated fallback rate, or weak cohort representation.
- **Ticket and trend:** a low-severity feature-quality change with no current decision impact, capacity cost growth, or a documentation gap.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="tradeoffs" title="8. Choose explicit trade-offs and define the next maturity step" %}

## The platform makes choices visible; it cannot remove them

| Decision | Default for this case | Cost of the choice | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Feature freshness | Hybrid online and batch features | More stores and consistency checks | Version definitions and declare stale states |
| Deployment | Shadow then canary | Slower promotion and more telemetry | Automate evidence collection and rollback |
| Governance | Central platform with team-owned models | Platform can become a bottleneck | Self-service templates, quotas, and clear ownership |
| Retraining | Evidence-triggered with human review | Some regressions need manual response | Predefine triggers, runbooks, and rollback authority |

### First 90 days

1. Establish the release record: artifact digest, feature version, data snapshot, evaluation report, owner, approval, and rollback target.
2. Make one critical model safe end to end: point-in-time data, online/offline feature checks, a shadow run, canary guardrails, and a tested rollback.
3. Add versioned outcome logging and slice dashboards before expanding to more model teams.
4. Productize the repeated controls only after teams have exercised them in incidents and can explain the remaining failure modes.

{% callout variant="tip" %}
### Case-study conclusion

MLOps maturity is not the number of tools in the platform. It is the ability to
answer, during an incident, which release served a decision, what inputs it used,
which guardrail failed, who owns the next action, and how to restore a compatible
state quickly.
{% /callout %}
{% /accordion-item %}

{% /accordion %}

